The expression

Φ(−dUdt)\Phi\left(-\frac{dU}{dt}\right)

has a clean physical interpretation:

Effective Output Rate=Φ×Energy Release Rate\boxed{ \text{Effective Output Rate} = \Phi \times \text{Energy Release Rate} }

where:

U(t)U(t) is stored energy, potential, or another conserved quantity.
−dUdt-\dfrac{dU}{dt} is the rate at which that quantity is being released.
Φ\Phi is a dimensionless weighting factor, efficiency, coupling coefficient, or utilization factor.
Example

Suppose a battery contains stored energy

U(t)=1000e−0.05t J.U(t)=1000e^{-0.05t}\ \text{J}.

Then

−dUdt=50e−0.05t W.-\frac{dU}{dt} = 50e^{-0.05t}\ \text{W}.

If only 80% of the released energy is converted into useful work,

Φ=0.8,\Phi=0.8,

then

Φ(−dUdt)=40e−0.05t W.\Phi\left(-\frac{dU}{dt}\right) = 40e^{-0.05t}\ \text{W}.

At t=0t=0,

40 W40\ \text{W}

of useful power is produced, while

10 W10\ \text{W}

is lost.

Physical analogies
System	UU	Φ\Phi	Φ(−dU/dt)\Phi(-dU/dt)Battery	Stored electrical energy	Electrical efficiency	Useful output power
Chemical reaction	Gibbs free energy	Reaction yield	Useful chemical work rate
Solar panel	Incident solar energy	Conversion efficiency	Electrical power output
Mechanical system	Potential energy	Transmission efficiency	Delivered mechanical power

Thus,

Φ(−dUdt)\boxed{\Phi\left(-\frac{dU}{dt}\right)}

can be interpreted as the useful power extracted from a reservoir whose stored potential UU is decreasing with time.
